      "text": "The Web Development section of the H2 Computing (9569) syllabus integrates client-side structure (HTML), presentation (CSS), server-side logic (Python/Flask), and data persistence (SQLite/MongoDB). \n\nTo master the practical papers, you must memorise the core boilerplate and adopt a systematic workflow. Exams rarely test web development in isolation; it is almost always the final task integrating database queries with a user interface.\n\nHere is the rundown of essential skills, memorisable code structures, and the simplest method to ace these tasks.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Essential Code Structures to Memorise\n\n#### A. The Minimal HTML Template\nMemorise this skeleton. It contains the required tags and the standard structure for a data-submission form and a data-display table.\n\n```html\n\u003c!doctype html\u003e\n\u003chtml\u003e\n\u003chead\u003e\n    \u003ctitle\u003eWeb App\u003c/title\u003e\n    \u003clink rel\u003d\"stylesheet\" href\u003d\"{{ url_for(\u0027static\u0027, filename\u003d\u0027style.css\u0027) }}\"\u003e\n\u003c/head\u003e\n\u003cbody\u003e\n    \u003c!-- Form Structure --\u003e\n    \u003cform method\u003d\"POST\" action\u003d\"/\"\u003e\n        Name: \u003cinput type\u003d\"text\" name\u003d\"username\"\u003e\n        \u003cinput type\u003d\"submit\" value\u003d\"Submit\"\u003e\n    \u003c/form\u003e\n\n    \u003c!-- Jinja2 Table Rendering --\u003e\n    {% if results %}\n    \u003ctable\u003e\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eHeader 1\u003c/th\u003e\n            \u003cth\u003eHeader 2\u003c/th\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        {% for row in results %}\n        \u003ctr\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e{{ row[0] }}\u003c/td\u003e\n            \u003ctd\u003e{{ row[1] }}\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003c/tr\u003e\n        {% endfor %}\n    \u003c/table\u003e\n    {% endif %}\n\u003c/body\u003e\n\u003c/html\u003e\n```\n\n#### B. The Universal Flask Boilerplate\nThis is the only Python structure you need to handle routing, form submission, and template rendering.\n\n```python\nfrom flask import Flask, render_template, request, redirect, url_for\nimport sqlite3 # or pymongo\n\napp \u003d Flask(__name__)\n\n@app.route(\u0027/\u0027, methods\u003d[\u0027GET\u0027, \u0027POST\u0027])\ndef index():\n    if request.method \u003d\u003d \u0027POST\u0027:\n        # 1. Retrieve Form Data\n        user_input \u003d request.form[\u0027username\u0027]\n        \n        # 2. Process Data / Query Database\n        conn \u003d sqlite3.connect(\u0027database.db\u0027)\n        cur \u003d conn.cursor()\n        cur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM table WHERE name \u003d ?\", (user_input,))\n        data \u003d cur.fetchall()\n        conn.close()\n        \n        # 3. Render Results\n        return render_template(\u0027index.html\u0027, results\u003ddata)\n    \n    # Render default page for GET request\n    return render_template(\u0027index.html\u0027, results\u003dNone)\n\nif __name__ \u003d\u003d \u0027__main__\u0027:\n    app.run(port\u003d5000)\n```\n\n#### C. File Uploads (Specific Syllabus Requirement)\nIf the question requires file uploads (e.g., uploading an MC or image):\n1.  **HTML Form:** Must include `enctype\u003d\"multipart/form-data\"` and `\u003cinput type\u003d\"file\" name\u003d\"file\"\u003e`.\n2.  **Flask Logic:** Access via `f \u003d request.files[\u0027file\u0027]` and save using `f.save(filepath)`.\n\n#### D. Core CSS (The Box Model \u0026 Selectors)\nKeep styling minimal unless specified. Memorise how to target elements, IDs (`#`), and classes (`.`).\n\n```css\n/* Center a block element, apply borders and padding */\n.container {\n    width: 800px;\n    margin-left: auto;\n    margin-right: auto;\n    padding: 20px;\n    border: 1px solid black;\n    background: #f0f0f0;\n}\n/* Table styling */\ntable, th, td {\n    border: 1px solid black;\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Necessary Skills\n\n*   **HTTP Methods:** Differentiate between `GET` (data in URL via `request.args`, used for viewing) and `POST` (data in request body via `request.form`, used for submitting/modifying).\n*   **Routing Variables:** Passing data via URLs using type converters (e.g., `@app.route(\u0027/user/\u003cint:id\u003e\u0027)`).\n*   **Jinja2 Syntax:** \n    *   Variables: `{{ variable }}`\n    *   Control flow: `{% if condition %}`, `{% elif condition %}`, `{% endif %}`\n    *   Loops: `{% for item in iterable %}`, `{% endfor %}`\n*   **Static Files:** Serving CSS/images from a `static/` folder using `{{ url_for(\u0027static\u0027, filename\u003d\u0027style.css\u0027) }}`.\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Simplest, Most Straightforward Method for Practical Tasks\n\nDuring the exam, do not overcomplicate the web application. Follow this linear, foolproof workflow:\n\n**Step 1: Directory Setup (2 minutes)**\nImmediately create your project folder. Inside it, create `app.py`, a `templates/` folder (for HTML files), and a `static/` folder (for CSS/images). Flask *will not work* if your HTML is outside the `templates` folder.\n\n**Step 2: Write the Flask Skeleton (5 minutes)**\nWrite the base Flask app with a single `@app.route(\u0027/\u0027)` returning a simple string (`return \"Hello\"`). Run it to verify the server starts and port 5000 is accessible.\n\n**Step 3: Build the HTML Form First (5 minutes)**\nCreate `index.html`. Write the bare minimum HTML to capture the required inputs. Ensure your `\u003cform\u003e` has `method\u003d\"POST\"` and every `\u003cinput\u003e` has a `name` attribute. Do not style it yet.\n\n**Step 4: Handle the POST Request \u0026 Database Query (10-15 minutes)**\nIn `app.py`, add `methods\u003d[\u0027GET\u0027, \u0027POST\u0027]` to your route. \nWrite the `if request.method \u003d\u003d \u0027POST\u0027:` block. Extract the variables using `request.form[\u0027name\u0027]`. Write your SQLite or MongoDB query using these variables. *Test this by printing the database results to the Python console.* Once the console prints the correct data, you know the backend works.\n\n**Step 5: Render the Data in HTML using Jinja2 (10 minutes)**\nPass the database results to `render_template`. In your HTML file, use a `{% for %}` loop to generate `\u003ctr\u003e` and `\u003ctd\u003e` tags for each record. \n\n**Step 6: Apply CSS Styling Last (5 minutes)**\nOnly once the data flows correctly from the form, to the database, and back to the screen, should you touch CSS. Create `style.css` in the `static` folder. Apply the required colours, table borders, and margins. \n\n**Pro-Tip for Exams:** If your CSS or Jinja2 syntax is breaking the page and time is running out, remove the CSS/Jinja and use Python string concatenation to return raw HTML directly from the Flask route (e.g., `return f\"\u003ch1\u003e{data[0]}\u003c/h1\u003e\"`). It is less elegant and technically non-standard for complex apps, but it secures functionality marks when you are stuck.",
