      "text": "In the context of the H2 Computing (9569) practical exam, the development environment is **standardised and local**. You do not need to worry about hosting your web app on the internet; the examiners evaluate your work directly from the files on your exam computer.\n\n### The Development Environment\nYou are provided with a dedicated exam laptop pre-installed with the necessary software. Based on past papers and standard exam setup, you will typically use:\n*   **Jupyter Notebook:** The primary interface for most programming tasks. You will save your work as `.ipynb` files.\n*   **IDLE:** Often used for writing and running the backend Python code (`app.py` or socket programs) because it is simple and lightweight.\n*   **Notepad++:** Used for writing HTML and CSS files. It is preferred for web development because it provides syntax highlighting, which makes spotting errors in your tags or style rules much easier than in a plain text editor.\n*   **Local Execution:** Your web app runs on your local machine (typically `127.0.0.1:5000`). You \"run\" the app by executing your `app.py` in IDLE or a terminal, then open your browser to the local address to test your work.\n\n### How to Organize Your Work\nWhen you develop a web application during the exam, you must follow the standard directory structure or the exam will not be able to find your files:\n1.  **Project Folder:** Create a folder for each specific web app task as instructed.\n2.  **`app.py`:** Your main Flask application code.\n3.  **`templates/` folder:** **Strictly** place all your `.html` files here. Flask looks for templates in this specific folder by default.\n4.  **`static/` folder:** **Strictly** place your `style.css` and images here. Linking them via `url_for(\u0027static\u0027, filename\u003d\u0027...\u0027)` ensures they are correctly served.\n\n### Strategy for \"Developing\" vs. \"Running\"\n*   **Development:** Use **Notepad++** to edit your HTML/CSS and **IDLE** to edit your `app.py`. Keep both open side-by-side.\n*   **Testing:** Save your files in Notepad++ and IDLE. Go to your Python IDLE window (or terminal) and run your `app.py`. If you make changes, **you must stop and re-run the Python script** for the changes to take effect in the browser.\n*   **Submission:** Ensure all files are saved in the correct folder structure as specified by the instructions. The examiners will check if your folder contains the necessary sub-directories (`templates/`, `static/`).\n\n**Key Takeaway:** You are not \"deploying\" a site; you are writing local files that the Flask server reads. As long as your folder structure is correct and your paths (using `url_for`) are relative to the project root, it will work perfectly on the exam machine.",
