      "text": "The message `* Running on http://127.0.0.1:5000` indicates that your Flask server is successfully running and waiting for requests. If Chrome says \"This site can\u0027t be reached,\" the issue is almost never the code itself—it is usually a **directory structure problem** or an **access/port conflict**.\n\nHere is how to troubleshoot and fix this, step-by-step.\n\n### 1. The Critical Folder Structure\nFlask expects a very specific layout. If your files are not placed exactly like this, the server will not be able to find your HTML templates or CSS files.\n\n**Ensure your project folder looks exactly like this:**\n```text\n/your_project_folder\n    ├── app.py              \u003c-- Your Python Flask code\n    ├── templates/          \u003c-- Folder MUST be named \"templates\" (plural)\n    │   └── index.html      \u003c-- Your HTML files go here\n    └── static/             \u003c-- Folder MUST be named \"static\"\n        └── style.css       \u003c-- Your CSS files go here\n```\n*   **Common Mistake:** Naming the folder `template` instead of `templates`. Flask looks for `templates` by default; if it’s named `template`, it won\u0027t find your files.\n\n### 2. Check for Port Conflicts\nIf you see an error in the terminal like `OSError: [Errno 98] Address already in use`, it means another program is already using port 5000. \n*   **The Fix:** Change the port in your `app.run()` call to something else, like 5001.\n    ```python\n    if __name__ \u003d\u003d \u0027__main__\u0027:\n        app.run(port\u003d5001)\n    ```\n*   Then, navigate to `http://127.0.0.1:5001` in your browser.\n\n### 3. Are You Using the Correct \"File Addresses\"?\nYou asked which parts of the code need specific file addresses. In Flask, you **never** use absolute file paths (like `C:\\Users\\Documents\\...`). Instead, use **Flask\u0027s helper functions** so the server always knows where to look:\n\n#### For HTML Files (Templates)\nYou do not use a file path. You use the filename inside `render_template`. Flask automatically looks inside the `templates/` folder.\n*   **Correct:** `return render_template(\u0027index.html\u0027)`\n*   **Incorrect:** `return render_template(\u0027templates/index.html\u0027)` (Flask is already looking there).\n\n#### For CSS/Images (Static Files)\nYou must use `url_for` to point to the `static/` folder. This generates the correct relative path for the browser.\n*   **In your HTML file:**\n    ```html\n    \u003clink rel\u003d\"stylesheet\" href\u003d\"{{ url_for(\u0027static\u0027, filename\u003d\u0027style.css\u0027) }}\"\u003e\n    ```\n*   This tells Flask: \"Look in the `static` folder for a file named `style.css`.\"\n\n### 4. How to Test Without the Browser\nIf you are still having trouble, test if the server is actually responding to the computer itself.\n1.  Open your **Command Prompt** or **Terminal**.\n2.  Type: `curl http://127.0.0.1:5000/`\n3.  If you see your HTML code printed in the terminal, the **server is working perfectly**, and the issue is likely a browser setting, an ad-blocker, or an \"AirPlay Receiver\" conflict (common on macOS) that prevents Chrome from connecting to port 5000.\n\n### Summary Checklist\n1.  **Check the folder names:** Is it `templates/` and `static/`?\n2.  **Verify the `app.py` location:** Is it in the root folder (outside `templates` and `static`)?\n3.  **Check browser address:** Are you typing `http://127.0.0.1:5000`? (Don\u0027t forget the `http://` prefix).\n4.  **Restart:** Stop the script in IDLE (Ctrl+C or close the window) and re-run `app.py`. Flask does not always \"auto-reload\" changes in the exam environment.",
