      "text": "Now that your foundational boilerplate works, you must expand your web development repertoire before tackling complex databases. Since your database skills are currently weak, **use mock data (Python lists and dictionaries) to master Flask and Jinja2 first.** This isolates web errors from database errors.\n\nHere is your 3-step progression strategy to master the remaining 9569 web development syllabus.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Master Dynamic Routes and Data Rendering (No DB Required)\nLearn to pass data through the URL and render complex Python data structures in HTML.\n\n**1. Variable Routes:**\nPass parameters directly through the URL (e.g., `http://127.0.0.1:5000/profile/john`).\n```python\n@app.route(\u0027/profile/\u003cusername\u003e\u0027)\ndef profile(username):\n    # In an exam, you would query the DB here using \u0027username\u0027\n    return render_template(\u0027profile.html\u0027, name\u003dusername)\n```\n\n**2. Jinja2 Looping with Mock Data:**\nCreate a list of dictionaries to simulate database rows (`fetchall()` or MongoDB `find()`). Master rendering this structure into an HTML table.\n```python\n@app.route(\u0027/students\u0027)\ndef students():\n    # Mock database output\n    mock_db \u003d [\n        {\u0027id\u0027: 1, \u0027name\u0027: \u0027Alice\u0027, \u0027grade\u0027: \u0027A\u0027},\n        {\u0027id\u0027: 2, \u0027name\u0027: \u0027Bob\u0027, \u0027grade\u0027: \u0027B\u0027}\n    ]\n    return render_template(\u0027students.html\u0027, records\u003dmock_db)\n```\n**`students.html` Boilerplate:**\n```html\n\u003ctable\u003e\n    \u003ctr\u003e\u003cth\u003eID\u003c/th\u003e\u003cth\u003eName\u003c/th\u003e\u003cth\u003eGrade\u003c/th\u003e\u003c/tr\u003e\n    {% for row in records %}\n    \u003ctr\u003e\n        \u003ctd\u003e{{ row[\u0027id\u0027] }}\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003ctd\u003e{{ row[\u0027name\u0027] }}\u003c/td\u003e\n        \u003ctd\u003e{{ row[\u0027grade\u0027] }}\u003c/td\u003e\n    \u003c/tr\u003e\n    {% endfor %}\n\u003c/table\u003e\n```\n\n### Step 2: Master File Uploads (Core Syllabus Requirement)\nFile uploads (e.g., uploading an MC or a receipt) are frequently tested. Memorise this exact boilerplate.\n\n**HTML Form (Crucial detail: `enctype`):**\n```html\n\u003cform method\u003d\"POST\" action\u003d\"/upload\" enctype\u003d\"multipart/form-data\"\u003e\n    \u003cinput type\u003d\"file\" name\u003d\"document\"\u003e\n    \u003cinput type\u003d\"submit\" value\u003d\"Upload\"\u003e\n\u003c/form\u003e\n```\n\n**Flask Logic (Crucial detail: `request.files`):**\n```python\nimport os\n\n@app.route(\u0027/upload\u0027, methods\u003d[\u0027POST\u0027])\ndef upload():\n    # 1. Retrieve the file object\n    uploaded_file \u003d request.files[\u0027document\u0027]\n    \n    # 2. Check if a file was actually selected\n    if uploaded_file.filename !\u003d \u0027\u0027:\n        # 3. Save it to a specific folder (e.g., \u0027static/\u0027)\n        filepath \u003d os.path.join(\u0027static\u0027, uploaded_file.filename)\n        uploaded_file.save(filepath)\n        return \"File uploaded successfully!\"\n    \n    return \"No file selected.\"\n```\n\n### Step 3: The \"Plug-and-Play\" Database Bridge\nOnce your Flask routes and HTML forms run flawlessly, replace your mock data with actual database queries. Memorise these two minimal blocks to plug directly into your `POST` logic.\n\n#### A. SQLite Boilerplate (Relational)\nUse this when the exam provides `.csv` files strictly structured with IDs and foreign keys.\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\n\n# 1. READ (e.g., Searching for a student)\nconn \u003d sqlite3.connect(\u0027school.db\u0027)\ncur \u003d conn.cursor()\ncur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM student WHERE name \u003d ?\", (search_input,))\nresults \u003d cur.fetchall() # Returns a list of tuples: [(1, \u0027Alice\u0027), (2, \u0027Bob\u0027)]\nconn.close()\n\n# 2. WRITE (e.g., Adding a late record)\nconn \u003d sqlite3.connect(\u0027school.db\u0027)\ncur \u003d conn.cursor()\ncur.execute(\"INSERT INTO latecoming (stu_id, date) VALUES (?, ?)\", (id_input, date_input))\nconn.commit() # DO NOT FORGET TO COMMIT\nconn.close()\n```\n\n#### B. PyMongo Boilerplate (NoSQL)\nUse this when the exam provides `.json` files or data with variable fields (e.g., some students have MCs, some do not).\n\n```python\nimport pymongo\n\n# 1. Connect\nclient \u003d pymongo.MongoClient(\"127.0.0.1\", 27017)\ndb \u003d client[\u0027school\u0027]\ncollection \u003d db[\u0027attendance\u0027]\n\n# 2. READ (e.g., Find absentees)\n# Returns an iterable cursor of dictionaries\nresults \u003d list(collection.find({\"status\": \"Absent\"})) \n\n# 3. WRITE (e.g., Insert new record)\nnew_record \u003d {\"name\": name_input, \"status\": \"Present\"}\ncollection.insert_one(new_record)\n```\n\n### Your Immediate Action Plan\n1. **Build a 3-page site with mock data:** Create a Home page (`/`), an Add Data page (`/add`, containing a POST form), and a View Data page (`/view`, displaying a hardcoded Python list as a table). \n2. **Implement an Image Upload:** Add an upload form that saves an image to your `static` folder, then displays that image on a webpage using `\u003cimg src\u003d\"{{ url_for(\u0027static\u0027, filename\u003duploaded_filename) }}\"\u003e`.\n3. **Integrate DB last:** Once the web logic flows perfectly, inject the SQLite or PyMongo boilerplates to replace the mock data.",
