      "text": "You are in an **excellent position** to start SQL/NoSQL. You have successfully navigated the \"Flask-HTML-Form\" barrier, which is the most common point where students get stuck.\n\n### Assessment of Readiness\n*   **Strengths:** You have mastered the \"Post-Redirect-Get\" pattern (even if you haven\u0027t implemented the redirect yet), you are correctly handling variable inputs from forms, and your file structure is clean and functional. \n*   **Weaknesses to address:** Your current logic has a \"UnboundLocalError\" trap. If a user visits `/search` via a `GET` request, your `search()` function attempts to return `render_template(..., results\u003ddata, category\u003dcategory, name\u003dname)`, but `data`, `category`, and `name` have not been defined yet.\n\n---\n\n### Immediate Fix: The \"Local Variable\" Trap\nIn your `search` function, if the user visits the page via `GET`, your function will crash because `data`, `category`, and `name` are not defined.\n\n**Refactor your `search` function to be \"safe\":**\n```python\n@app.route(\u0027/search\u0027, methods\u003daccepted)\ndef search():\n    # Define defaults to prevent UnboundLocalError\n    data \u003d None\n    category \u003d \"N/A\"\n    name \u003d \"N/A\"\n    \n    if request.method \u003d\u003d \u0027POST\u0027:\n        category \u003d request.form.get(\u0027category\u0027, \u0027N/A\u0027)\n        name \u003d request.form.get(\u0027name\u0027, \u0027N/A\u0027)\n        # ... database logic ...\n        \n    return render_template(\u0027results.html\u0027, results\u003ddata, category\u003dcategory, name\u003dname)\n```\n*Note: Using `.get()` on the form object is safer than `request.form[\u0027name\u0027]` because it won\u0027t crash if the key is missing.*\n\n---\n\n### Roadmap: Transitioning to SQL and NoSQL\n\nSince you understand how to move data from a form to Python, the rest of the syllabus is just \"swapping\" the data handling part.\n\n#### 1. SQL (The \"Relational\" Way)\n*   **Concepts to master:** `JOIN` queries. In the exam, you won\u0027t just query one table. You will need to join `Student` and `Latecoming` tables using `stu_id`.\n*   **Boilerplate to memorise:** \n    ```python\n    cur.execute(\"\"\"\n        SELECT student.name, latecoming.reason \n        FROM student \n        JOIN latecoming ON student.stu_id \u003d latecoming.stu_id\n        WHERE student.civics_class \u003d ?\n    \"\"\", (civics_class,))\n    ```\n\n#### 2. NoSQL (The \"Document\" Way)\n*   **Concepts to master:** Hierarchical data. NoSQL (MongoDB) handles JSON-like structures. You must learn how to handle records where some fields might be missing (e.g., if a student isn\u0027t absent, there is no `reason` field).\n*   **Boilerplate to memorise:**\n    ```python\n    # Searching with criteria\n    results \u003d list(collection.find({\"cg\": \"25S02X\", \"status\": \"Absent\"}))\n    ```\n\n#### 3. Integration (The Exam Pattern)\nThe exam will almost always follow this 3-step loop:\n1.  **Form Input:** User selects `class` or `date` on your HTML form.\n2.  **Query:** Your Flask route receives that input and crafts the `SELECT` (SQL) or `find` (NoSQL) query.\n3.  **Looping Display:** You pass the resulting list of dictionaries/tuples into Jinja2, which loops through them to populate the HTML table.\n\n### Your Final \"Mastery\" Checklist\nBefore you dive into the database chapters:\n1.  **Can you write a query that joins two tables?** (Practice this in a standalone Python script).\n2.  **Can you display MongoDB results?** (Remember: MongoDB results come back as a list of dictionaries, so in Jinja2 you use `{{ item[\u0027name\u0027] }}` instead of `{{ item[0] }}`).\n3.  **Are you comfortable with Python `try-except` blocks?** Databases fail often (network down, table missing). Your app should catch these to avoid showing the user a ugly traceback.\n\n**You are ready.** My suggestion: Start the SQL chapter next. The logic is more rigid than MongoDB, which makes it easier to debug when you are learning the ropes. Do you want the specific SQL \"Cheat Codes\" for the typical exam-style queries?",
