      "text": "To \"conquer\" the SQL sections, you need to understand that the Python code you write is simply a **messenger**. You are sending SQL commands (strings) to a database engine, which performs the work and sends the results back to Python.\n\nWe will focus on the **Lifecycle of a Query**: `Connect` -\u003e `Cursor` -\u003e `Execute` -\u003e `Fetch` -\u003e `Close`.\n\n### 1. The Fundamental Structure\nIn every SQL task, you will follow this exact pattern. Memorise this structure:\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\n\n# 1. Connect (The database is just a file on your disk)\nconn \u003d sqlite3.connect(\u0027college.db\u0027)\n\n# 2. Create a Cursor (The tool used to \u0027point\u0027 at the database)\ncur \u003d conn.cursor()\n\n# 3. Execute (Send the SQL command)\ncur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM Student\")\n\n# 4. Fetch (Retrieve the results from the cursor)\nrows \u003d cur.fetchall()\n\n# 5. Clean up (Always close to prevent data corruption)\nconn.close()\n\n# 6. Display (Working with the results in Python)\nfor row in rows:\n    print(row)\n```\n\n### 2. Step-by-Step Mastery Strategy\n\n#### Phase 1: Exploration (Direct SQL Execution)\nBefore you write complex logic, you need to see how the data looks. Use these three commands in your terminal or a test script to \"feel\" the data:\n*   `SELECT * FROM table_name;` (See everything)\n*   `SELECT name, civics_class FROM Student;` (See only specific columns)\n*   `SELECT * FROM Student WHERE civics_class \u003d \u002725S02X\u0027;` (Filtering)\n\n#### Phase 2: Python Integration (The \"Bridge\")\nYou must learn to use **parameterized queries**. This is non-negotiable for the exam. Never inject user input directly into a string (e.g., `WHERE name \u003d \u0027 + user_input + \u0027`). It is insecure and will lose you marks.\n\n**Always use the `?` placeholder:**\n```python\n# The \u0027?\u0027 tells SQLite to safely plug in the variable\ncur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM Student WHERE civics_class \u003d ?\", (user_input,))\n```\n\n#### Phase 3: Extraction \u0026 Transformation\nYou will eventually need to convert \"database rows\" into \"data your HTML can use.\"\n*   `fetchall()` returns a **list of tuples**.\n*   A tuple is accessed by index (e.g., `row[0]` is the first column).\n*   **Pro-Tip:** If you need to map this to an HTML table, convert the tuple into a dictionary or list of dictionaries within your Python route before passing it to `render_template`.\n\n---\n\n### Task: Build your \"SQL Playground\"\nTo familiarise yourself, create a file called `test_db.py` and run this code. This will simulate a database in your computer\u0027s RAM.\n\n```python\nimport sqlite3\n\n# Connect to a temporary in-memory database\nconn \u003d sqlite3.connect(\u0027:memory:\u0027)\ncur \u003d conn.cursor()\n\n# Create a table\ncur.execute(\"CREATE TABLE Student (id INTEGER, name TEXT, class TEXT)\")\n\n# Insert sample data\ncur.execute(\"INSERT INTO Student VALUES (1, \u0027Alice\u0027, \u002725S02X\u0027)\")\ncur.execute(\"INSERT INTO Student VALUES (2, \u0027Bob\u0027, \u002725S02Y\u0027)\")\nconn.commit() # IMPORTANT: Save changes to the DB\n\n# Task: Try to fetch and print only the name of the student in \u002725S02X\u0027\ncur.execute(\"SELECT name FROM Student WHERE class \u003d ?\", (\u002725S02X\u0027,))\nresult \u003d cur.fetchone() # Fetchone retrieves only one record\nprint(f\"The student is: {result[0]}\")\n\nconn.close()\n```\n\n### Why this works:\n1.  **`:memory:`**: This creates a database that vanishes when the program stops. It’s perfect for testing because you can\u0027t \"break\" your actual `college.db` file.\n2.  **`commit()`**: You will forget this constantly. Any `INSERT`, `UPDATE`, or `DELETE` requires `conn.commit()`. Only `SELECT` queries don\u0027t need it.\n\n**Does this structure make sense? Once you run this and see the output in your console, the next step will be to learn how to read your `STUDENT.csv` file and `INSERT` that data into a real database file.**"