      "createTime": "2026-06-26T10:18:16.360Z"
    }, {
      "text": "In an A-Level practical exam, **Jupyter Notebook is the standard environment** for tasks involving database creation and file processing. IDLE is usually reserved for standalone scripts or server/client programs.\n\nSince Jupyter Notebook is built on top of Python, it handles file I/O and SQLite3 exactly the same way as IDLE. However, Jupyter has the advantage of allowing you to check your data visually in \"cells\" after each step.\n\n---\n\n### Step-by-Step: Importing CSV Data into SQLite in Jupyter\n\nFor this example, assume you have a file `STUDENT.csv` with headers `stu_id, name, civics_class, handphone`.\n\n#### Step 1: Import necessary libraries\nIn a new cell, import the modules.\n```python\nimport sqlite3\nimport csv\n```\n\n#### Step 2: Establish the Database Connection\nCreate the database and the table. It is good practice to include `DROP TABLE IF EXISTS` so you can re-run this cell multiple times without `IntegrityError` issues.\n```python\nconn \u003d sqlite3.connect(\u0027college.db\u0027)\ncur \u003d conn.cursor()\n\n# Create table with correct data types\ncur.execute(\"\"\"\n    CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS Student (\n        stu_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY,\n        name TEXT,\n        civics_class TEXT,\n        handphone TEXT\n    )\n\"\"\")\nconn.commit()\n```\n\n#### Step 3: Read and Process the CSV File\nUse the built-in `csv` module. This is the \"A-Level standard\" way to parse files.\n```python\nwith open(\u0027STUDENT.csv\u0027, \u0027r\u0027) as file:\n    reader \u003d csv.reader(file)\n    next(reader) # Skip the header row so we don\u0027t try to insert the column names as data\n    \n    for row in reader:\n        # row is now a list like [\u002725076\u0027, \u0027John Doe\u0027, \u002725S02X\u0027, \u002791234567\u0027]\n        cur.execute(\"\"\"\n            INSERT OR IGNORE INTO Student (stu_id, name, civics_class, handphone)\n            VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?)\n        \"\"\", row)\n\nconn.commit()\nprint(\"Import complete!\")\n```\n\n#### Step 4: Verify the Data\nThis is why Jupyter is superior to IDLE. In a new cell, perform a query to see if the data made it in.\n```python\ncur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM Student LIMIT 5\")\nprint(cur.fetchall())\nconn.close()\n```\n\n---\n\n### The \"Exam Strategy\" for File Processing\n\n1.  **Skip the Headers:** Never forget `next(reader)`. If your CSV file has a header row (e.g., `stu_id, name...`), failing to skip it will cause your `INSERT` to fail or insert invalid data into your table.\n2.  **Use `INSERT OR IGNORE`:** In exams, you might re-run your import script multiple times while debugging. Using `INSERT OR IGNORE` prevents your program from crashing if you accidentally try to import the same data twice.\n3.  **Data Cleaning:** Sometimes CSV files have empty spaces. If you find your data isn\u0027t matching, use `.strip()` on the fields:\n    ```python\n    for row in reader:\n        cleaned_row \u003d [field.strip() for field in row]\n        cur.execute(\"INSERT ...\", cleaned_row)\n    ```\n4.  **Handling `.txt` vs `.csv`:** \n    *   If the file is **CSV**: Use the `csv` module (as shown above).\n    *   If the file is **TXT (delimited)**: If it uses commas, it\u0027s just a CSV in disguise—use the `csv` module. If it uses other delimiters (e.g., `|` or `;`), use `csv.reader(file, delimiter\u003d\u0027|\u0027)`.\n\n### Why this is the \"Simplest Method\"\nYou don\u0027t need complex Pandas or library-heavy imports. The `csv` module is standard library, stable, and exactly what the examiners expect to see. It is lightweight, fast, and less prone to environment-specific errors than more complex data science tools.\n\n**Try this with one of your existing files.** If you get a `FileNotFoundError`, check that your `.csv` file is in the **same directory** as your Jupyter Notebook file (`.ipynb`). That is the most common error students face!",
