      "text": "To master NoSQL for the 9569 syllabus, you must stop thinking in \"tables\" and start thinking in \"dictionaries.\" MongoDB stores data as **BSON documents** (which are identical to Python dictionaries).\n\nHere is your comprehensive guide to connecting and performing CRUD (Create, Read, Update, Delete) operations using `pymongo`.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Connecting to MongoDB\nThe connection pattern is different from SQLite. You connect to a `MongoClient` instance, select a database, and then select a collection.\n\n```python\nimport pymongo\n\n# 1. Connect to the local MongoDB server\nclient \u003d pymongo.MongoClient(\"mongodb://localhost:27017/\")\n\n# 2. Select (or create) the database\ndb \u003d client[\u0027college_db\u0027]\n\n# 3. Select (or create) the collection (the equivalent of a table)\ncollection \u003d db[\u0027student\u0027]\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. The CRUD Boilerplate\n\n#### A. CREATE (Inserting Data)\nIn NoSQL, you don\u0027t use `INSERT INTO...VALUES`. You simply pass a **Python dictionary**.\n\n```python\n# Inserting one record\nstudent_data \u003d {\n    \"stu_id\": 25076,\n    \"name\": \"John Doe\",\n    \"civics_class\": \"25S02X\"\n}\ncollection.insert_one(student_data)\n\n# Inserting multiple records (from a list of dicts)\nstudents_list \u003d [\n    {\"stu_id\": 25077, \"name\": \"Alice\"},\n    {\"stu_id\": 25078, \"name\": \"Bob\"}\n]\ncollection.insert_many(students_list)\n```\n\n#### B. READ (Querying Data)\nThis is where the syntax changes most significantly from SQL.\n\n```python\n# 1. Find all documents\nall_students \u003d list(collection.find())\n\n# 2. Find with criteria (The SQL WHERE clause)\n# Find all students in class \u002725S02X\u0027\nresults \u003d list(collection.find({\"civics_class\": \"25S02X\"}))\n\n# 3. Projection (Selecting only specific fields)\n# 1 \u003d show, 0 \u003d hide. _id is shown by default unless set to 0.\nresults \u003d list(collection.find({\"civics_class\": \"25S02X\"}, {\"name\": 1, \"_id\": 0}))\n```\n\n#### C. UPDATE (Modifying Data)\nYou must use the `$set` operator. **If you forget `$set`, you will overwrite the entire document with the new data.**\n\n```python\n# Update the name of a specific student\ncollection.update_one(\n    {\"stu_id\": 25076},         # The FILTER (Which record?)\n    {\"$set\": {\"name\": \"Johnny\"}} # The UPDATE (What to change?)\n)\n```\n\n#### D. DELETE (Removing Data)\n```python\n# Delete one record matching the filter\ncollection.delete_one({\"stu_id\": 25076})\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. The \"Exam-Safe\" Workflow\nA-Level practicals often ask you to import JSON/CSV data into MongoDB. Here is how you transform that data efficiently:\n\n```python\nimport json\n\n# Example: Importing from a JSON file\nwith open(\u0027attendance.json\u0027, \u0027r\u0027) as file:\n    data \u003d json.load(file) # Loads data as a list of dictionaries\n    \n    # Clean import: clear existing data if required by task\n    collection.delete_many({}) \n    \n    # Bulk insert\n    collection.insert_many(data)\n```\n\n---\n\n### Key Comparisons for Your Exam\n\n| Feature | SQL (SQLite) | NoSQL (MongoDB) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Data Container** | Table | Collection |\n| **Record Unit** | Row | Document (Dictionary) |\n| **Filtering** | `WHERE col \u003d \u0027val\u0027` | `{\"col\": \"val\"}` |\n| **Updating** | `UPDATE table SET x\u003dy` | `update_one(filter, {\"$set\": {\"x\": y}})` |\n| **Joining** | `JOIN` statements | (Usually avoided) Embedding or logic in Python |\n\n### Final Advice for \"Mastery\"\n1.  **Always use `list()` with `find()`**: `collection.find()` returns a cursor object. If you try to print it directly, you\u0027ll get a memory address. `list(collection.find(...))` converts it into a readable list of dictionaries.\n2.  **Dictionaries are Case Sensitive**: Unlike SQL keywords (`SELECT`), MongoDB query keys are case-sensitive. `{\"civics_class\": \"25S02X\"}` is different from `{\"Civics_Class\": \"25S02X\"}`.\n3.  **No `commit()` needed**: Unlike SQLite, `pymongo` operations are usually committed automatically. You don\u0027t need `conn.commit()`.\n\n**Ready to try this?** Create a Python script that connects to a local MongoDB instance, inserts 3 students as dictionaries, and then writes a `find` query to retrieve students from a specific class. If you get a `ServerSelectionTimeoutError`, it means your MongoDB service isn\u0027t running on your computer.",
