      "text": "In MongoDB, you **do not have a `JOIN` command**. Because MongoDB is NoSQL, it handles relationships differently. You have two ways to solve this: **Embedding** (the preferred NoSQL way) or **Linking** (using `$lookup`).\n\nSince you are practicing for the 9569 syllabus, you should know the **`$lookup`** method, which is the MongoDB equivalent of a SQL `INNER JOIN`.\n\n### 1. The MongoDB `$lookup` (The \"Join\" Equivalent)\nIf your `student` collection and `late` collection are separate, you use an **Aggregation Pipeline**. This \"looks up\" data from another collection based on a shared field (e.g., `stu_id`).\n\n```python\npipeline \u003d [\n    # 1. Match the students you are looking for\n    {\"$match\": {\"civics_class\": query}}, \n    \n    # 2. Join with the \u0027late\u0027 collection\n    {\"$lookup\": {\n        \"from\": \"late\",            # The other collection\n        \"localField\": \"stu_id\",    # Field in \u0027student\u0027 collection\n        \"foreignField\": \"stu_id\",  # Field in \u0027late\u0027 collection\n        \"as\": \"late_records\"       # New array field added to each student document\n    }},\n    \n    # 3. Keep only students who actually have late records\n    {\"$match\": {\"late_records\": {\"$ne\": []}}}\n]\n\n# Run the aggregation\nfor student in collection.aggregate(pipeline):\n    print(student[\u0027name\u0027]) # This now only prints students who have late records\n```\n\n### 2. Why your previous approach resulted in duplicates\nIn your original code, you queried the `late` collection directly. If a student was late 5 times, there are 5 documents in the `late` collection with that `stu_id`. That is why you were getting the student\u0027s name printed 5 times.\n\n### 3. The \"NoSQL\" Best Practice: Denormalization\nIn the exam, if you find `$lookup` too complex to remember, you can use the **NoSQL approach: Denormalization**. \n\nInstead of two collections, store the late records **inside** the student document. \n\n**Example Student Document:**\n```json\n{\n    \"stu_id\": 25076,\n    \"name\": \"Alice\",\n    \"late_records\": [\n        {\"date\": \"2024-09-13\", \"reason\": \"Medical\"},\n        {\"date\": \"2024-09-15\", \"reason\": \"Traffic\"}\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n**Querying this is much easier:**\n```python\n# Find students who have at least one late record\nfor student in collection.find({\"late_records.0\": {\"$exists\": True}}):\n    print(student[\u0027name\u0027])\n```\n\n### Recommendation for your Exam Readiness\n*   **If the paper gives you two separate CSVs/JSONs:** They expect you to use **`$lookup`** (Aggregation Pipeline). It is the most \"correct\" way to handle relational data in NoSQL.\n*   **If you are building the database from scratch:** Use **Embedding** (putting late records inside the student document). It makes your life 10x easier because you avoid joins entirely.\n\n**Try applying the `$lookup` pipeline to your `student` and `late` collections.** It is the single most important NoSQL skill for the 9569 paper. \n\n**Does the concept of an \"Aggregation Pipeline\" (a list of stages) make sense to you, or would you like a breakdown of how the stages work?**",
