      "text": "In the context of the H2 Computing (9569) syllabus and practical exams, you **should** use `row_factory`, and here is why: it is the single most effective way to make your code more readable, less prone to index errors, and \"exam-proof.\"\n\n### 1. What is `row_factory`?\nBy default, `sqlite3` returns rows as **tuples** (e.g., `(25076, \u0027John Doe\u0027, \u002725S02X\u0027, \u002791234567\u0027)`). This forces you to remember the column order (is `name` at `row[1]` or `row[2]`?). If you change your SQL query, you have to rewrite all your index numbers.\n\n`row_factory \u003d sqlite3.Row` turns those tuples into **row objects** that behave like dictionaries.\n\n### 2. The Difference\n**Without `row_factory` (The hard way):**\n```python\ncur.execute(\"SELECT stu_id, name FROM student\")\nrow \u003d cur.fetchone()\nprint(row[1]) # You have to remember \u0027name\u0027 is index 1\n```\n\n**With `row_factory` (The exam-ready way):**\n```python\nconn.row_factory \u003d sqlite3.Row # This is the magic line\ncur \u003d conn.cursor()\ncur.execute(\"SELECT stu_id, name FROM student\")\nrow \u003d cur.fetchone()\nprint(row[\u0027name\u0027]) # Much clearer and safer!\n```\n\n### 3. Why you should use it in the exam\n1.  **Readability:** `row[\u0027name\u0027]` is self-documenting code. When you are under 2 hours of pressure, `row[4]` is a mystery, but `row[\u0027UnitPrice\u0027]` tells you exactly what you are doing.\n2.  **Safety:** If the exam question asks you to change the order of your columns in the `SELECT` statement, your old code (using indices like `row[1]`) will break and return the wrong data. Code using `row[\u0027name\u0027]` will keep working perfectly.\n3.  **NoSQL Consistency:** Since you are learning MongoDB, where results come back as **dictionaries**, using `sqlite3.Row` makes your SQL code look and feel like your MongoDB code. This reduces the \"mental context switching\" you need to do during the exam.\n\n### 4. How to implement it\nYou must set it **immediately after** connecting to the database, before you create your cursor:\n\n```python\nconn \u003d sqlite3.connect(\"database.db\")\nconn.row_factory \u003d sqlite3.Row  # Set this!\ncur \u003d conn.cursor()\n\n# Now every row fetched is a dictionary-like object\nfor row in cur.fetchall():\n    print(row[\u0027name\u0027]) \n```\n\n### Final Verdict on Readiness\nYou are now at a point where you understand the **what** (Flask routes, SQL/NoSQL queries, file parsing) and the **how** (boilerplates, PRG pattern, row_factory). \n\n**You do not need more theory.** You need to practice under time pressure. The next logical step is to simulate an exam paper:\n1.  Take a past year\u0027s practical paper (or a timed practice document).\n2.  Try to complete one full \"Task\" (Database creation + Flask integration) in **under 45 minutes.**\n\nIf you can do that, you are fully prepared for the practical assessment. Do you want to try an exam-style prompt now to see if you can tie all these components together?",
