      "text": "In H2 Computing (9569) practical exams, JSON (JavaScript Object Notation) is used exclusively as a lightweight format for data interchange. It is highly tested because **JSON maps perfectly to Python dictionaries/lists and MongoDB documents** [4].\n\nHere is your concise, exam-focused crash course on JSON handling.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Four Essential Functions\nPython\u0027s built-in `json` library uses a simple naming convention. The \"s\" in the function name stands for **String**.\n\n| Direction | Action on a **File** | Action on a **String** |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Read (Deserialize)** | `json.load(file_obj)` | `json.loads(string_obj)` |\n| **Write (Serialize)** | `json.dump(data, file_obj)` | `json.dumps(data)` |\n\n*Note: In the practical paper, you will almost exclusively use the file-based functions (`json.load` and `json.dump`).*\n\n---\n\n### 2. Python/JSON Type Conversions\nPython automatically translates types during serialization and deserialization:\n\n| JSON Type | Python Type |\n| :--- | :--- |\n| `object` (e.g. `{\"key\": \"val\"}`) | `dict` |\n| `array` (e.g. `[1, 2, 3]`) | `list` |\n| `true` / `false` | `True` / `False` |\n| `null` | `None` |\n\n---\n\n### 3. Classic Exam Boilerplate 1: Seeding MongoDB from a JSON File\nThis is the most common NoSQL starter task (as seen in the 2025 RI Timed Practice) [4].\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport pymongo\n\nclient \u003d pymongo.MongoClient(\"mongodb://localhost:27017/\")\ndb \u003d client[\u0027college\u0027]\ncollection \u003d db[\u0027attendance\u0027]\n\n# Reset the collection\ncollection.delete_many({})\n\n# Load and Seed\nwith open(\u0027attendance.json\u0027, \u0027r\u0027) as file:\n    # json.load reads the file and parses it directly into a Python list of dicts\n    data \u003d json.load(file) \n    \n    # Bulk insert into MongoDB\n    collection.insert_many(data)\n\nprint(f\"Successfully seeded {len(data)} records.\")\nclient.close()\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Classic Exam Boilerplate 2: Writing Database Results to a JSON File\nIf the paper asks you to export query results back into a JSON file, use `json.dump`.\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport sqlite3\n\nconn \u003d sqlite3.connect(\u0027database.db\u0027)\nconn.row_factory \u003d sqlite3.Row\ncur \u003d conn.cursor()\n\ncur.execute(\"SELECT * FROM student WHERE Class_Group \u003d ?\", (\"25S02X\",))\nrows \u003d cur.fetchall()\n\n# Convert sqlite3.Row objects to standard Python dictionaries\nstudent_list \u003d [dict(row) for row in rows]\n\n# Write to file with clean indentation (makes it readable for examiners)\nwith open(\u0027class_list.json\u0027, \u0027w\u0027) as file:\n    json.dump(student_list, file, indent\u003d4)\n\nconn.close()\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Critical Exam Trap: The MongoDB `ObjectId` Error\nIf you retrieve documents from MongoDB and attempt to write them straight to a JSON file using `json.dump()`, Python will crash:\n`TypeError: Object of type ObjectId is not JSON serializable`\n\n**Why:** Every MongoDB document has a hidden `_id` field of type `bson.objectid.ObjectId`, which standard Python JSON cannot parse.\n\n**How to fix it (2 methods):**\n\n*   **Method A: Project out the `_id` during your query (Recommended for Exams):**\n    ```python\n    # Tell MongoDB not to return the _id field\n    results \u003d list(collection.find({\"cg\": \"25S02X\"}, {\"_id\": 0}))\n    \n    with open(\u0027output.json\u0027, \u0027w\u0027) as file:\n        json.dump(results, file, indent\u003d4) # Will not crash\n    ```\n*   **Method B: Convert the `_id` to a string before dumping:**\n    ```python\n    results \u003d list(collection.find({\"cg\": \"25S02X\"}))\n    \n    for doc in results:\n        doc[\u0027_id\u0027] \u003d str(doc[\u0027_id\u0027]) # Convert ObjectId instance to a normal string\n        \n    with open(\u0027output.json\u0027, \u0027w\u0027) as file:\n        json.dump(results, file, indent\u003d4)\n    ```",
