      "text": "You already know how web browsers and servers communicate because you have mastered Flask. Flask is simply a massive, automated program that manages network connections for you. \n\n**Socket programming** is stripping away Flask and building the raw network pipes yourself. Instead of dealing with HTTP `GET`/`POST` requests or HTML, you are sending raw **bytes** back and forth between two Python scripts.\n\nHere is your exam-focused crash course on Sockets.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Core Concept: Server vs. Client\nA socket connection requires two endpoints:\n*   **The Server:** Claims a specific \"Port\" on a machine and waits (listens) for someone to call.\n*   **The Client:** Needs to know the Server\u0027s IP address and Port to initiate the call.\n\n*Note: For exams, the IP address is almost always `\u0027127.0.0.1\u0027` (localhost), and you make up a port number (e.g., `12345`).*\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Standard Boilerplates\nMemorise these two sequences. They are the foundation of every socket question.\n\n#### The Server Pipeline (`bind` ➔ `listen` ➔ `accept`)\n```python\nimport socket\n\n# 1. Create the raw socket\nlisten_socket \u003d socket.socket()\n\n# 2. Bind it to an IP and Port (Note the double parentheses for the tuple)\nlisten_socket.bind((\u0027127.0.0.1\u0027, 12345))\n\n# 3. Start listening for incoming requests\nlisten_socket.listen()\nprint(\"Waiting for a client...\")\n\n# 4. Accept a connection (This line blocks/pauses the script until a client connects)\nclient_socket, address \u003d listen_socket.accept()\nprint(f\"Connected to {address}\")\n\n# --- Data Transfer Happens Here ---\n\n# 5. Clean up\nclient_socket.close()\nlisten_socket.close()\n```\n\n#### The Client Pipeline (`connect`)\n```python\nimport socket\n\n# 1. Create the raw socket\nmy_socket \u003d socket.socket()\n\n# 2. Connect directly to the Server\u0027s IP and Port\nmy_socket.connect((\u0027127.0.0.1\u0027, 12345))\n\n# --- Data Transfer Happens Here ---\n\n# 3. Clean up\nmy_socket.close()\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Data Transfer: The \"Byte\" Rule\nSockets cannot send Python strings, lists, or dictionaries. They only send **bytes**. You must manually encode strings before sending and decode them after receiving.\n\n*   **Sending:** `socket.sendall(\"Hello\".encode())` or `socket.sendall(b\"Hello\")`\n*   **Receiving:** `data \u003d socket.recv(1024).decode()` \n\n---\n\n### 4. The \"Protocol\" Trap (Crucial for Exams)\nBecause networks can split or clump data, `recv(1024)` might grab half a message or two messages glued together. To prevent this, you must establish a **Protocol**—a rule for how messages end.\n\n**The standard exam protocol uses the newline character `\\n` as an end-of-message marker.** \n\nYou must use a `while` loop to keep receiving chunks until you detect the `\\n`. Memorise this block:\n\n```python\n# Sending with a protocol\nclient_socket.sendall(b\"My message goes here\\n\")\n\n# Receiving with a protocol (Exam-proof method)\ndata \u003d b\u0027\u0027\nwhile b\u0027\\n\u0027 not in data:\n    data +\u003d client_socket.recv(1024)\n\nmessage \u003d data.decode().strip() # .strip() removes the \u0027\\n\u0027\nprint(message)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Exam Application: The \"Turn-Based\" Loop\nPractical socket questions almost never ask for a simple \"send one message\" script. They ask you to build a **turn-based game** (like Hangman or Battleship).\n\nTo do this, you wrap your send/receive logic in an `while True:` loop. \n*   If the game is **symmetrical** (e.g., Chat app), both scripts have the exact same loop, just with `send` and `recv` swapped.\n*   If the game is **asymmetrical** (e.g., Server generates a secret word, Client guesses it), the Server handles the database/logic, and the Client acts as a dumb terminal.\n\n**Example: Asymmetrical Game Loop (Server Side)**\n```python\nwhile True:\n    # 1. Receive client\u0027s guess\n    data \u003d b\u0027\u0027\n    while b\u0027\\n\u0027 not in data:\n        data +\u003d client_socket.recv(1024)\n    guess \u003d data.decode().strip()\n    \n    # 2. Process logic (Check win/loss)\n    if guess \u003d\u003d \"QUIT\":\n        break\n    elif guess \u003d\u003d secret_word:\n        client_socket.sendall(b\"WIN\\n\")\n        break\n    else:\n        client_socket.sendall(b\"WRONG\\n\")\n\nclient_socket.close()\n```\n\n### Readiness Assessment\nYou already know how to write game logic and handle data. Socket programming is just wrapping that logic between `recv()` and `sendall()`. \n\nTo test your readiness, can you conceptualize how you would implement the **Client Side** of the loop shown above? (i.e., How would the client prompt the user, send the guess, and handle the \"WIN\" or \"WRONG\" response?)",
