      "text": "Based on a thorough review of your cheatsheet, the provided syllabus notes (Web Dev, Networks, Databases, Sockets), and the **2024 Year 6 Theory Timed Practice**, your current cheatsheet is heavily skewed towards *practical programming execution* but is critically missing the **\"Compare \u0026 Contrast\" definitions and architectural theory** required for Paper 1 (Theory).\n\nYou have correctly identified that your Database section is lacking, but there are also massive blind spots in Networking, Cybersecurity, and Web Architecture. \n\nHere is the rundown of the gaps you need to bridge, categorized by your urgency labels (Red, Yellow, Green).\n\n---\n\n### 🔴 RED: Requires Urgent Attention (High Weightage in Theory)\n\n**1. Databases: SQL vs NoSQL (Tested in Theory Q3)**\nYour cheatsheet admits this section is empty. The theory paper explicitly asks you to define differences and justify when to use which.\n*   **Missing Content:** \n    *   **SQL (Relational):** Structured rows/columns, predefined schema, vertical scalability (scaling up hardware). Ensures strict data integrity (ACID). Best for complex relationships (e.g., booking systems).\n    *   **NoSQL (MongoDB):** Flexible/dynamic schema (JSON-like documents), horizontal scalability (adding more servers). Best for diverse data types (e.g., reviews, varying item lists) and rapid real-time scaling.\n    *   **Table Definitions:** You must know how to translate an ER Diagram into 3NF text format: `TableName(Attribute1, Attribute2)` with Primary Keys underlined and Foreign Keys dashed-underlined.\n\n**2. Networking: The TCP/IP Model \u0026 Data Flow (Tested in Theory Q5 \u0026 Q6)**\nYour network section covers IP classes and subnets but misses the core theoretical backbone of the internet.\n*   **Missing Content:**\n    *   **The 4 TCP/IP Layers:** Application (HTTP), Transport (TCP/UDP, Ports), Network (IP Addresses, Routing), Data Link (MAC Addresses, hardware delivery).\n    *   **Connectionless vs Connection-Oriented:** \n        *   *Connection-oriented (TCP):* Reliable, ordered delivery. Used for web browsing, file transfers.\n        *   *Connectionless (UDP):* Fast, independent packet delivery without dedicated connections. Used for video streaming, real-time gaming.\n    *   **DNS Resolution Process:** You need the exact steps: Browser Cache $\\rightarrow$ Local ISP Resolver $\\rightarrow$ Root Server $\\rightarrow$ TLD Server (.com, .sg) $\\rightarrow$ Authoritative Server.\n\n**3. Cybersecurity \u0026 Malware Types (Tested in Theory Q6)**\nYour cheatsheet lists defense methods but lacks the *offensive definitions* the exam requires you to know.\n*   **Missing Content:** You must know the exact differences between:\n    *   **Virus:** Attaches to files, requires user action to spread.\n    *   **Worm:** Self-replicates and spreads automatically across networks without user action.\n    *   **Trojan:** Disguises itself as legitimate software to create backdoors.\n    *   **Ransomware:** Encrypts files and demands payment.\n    *   **Spyware:** Silently logs keystrokes/passwords.\n*   **Non-malware threats:** DDoS (flooding servers with traffic), Phishing (fake emails stealing credentials).\n\n---\n\n### 🟡 YELLOW: Needs Time to Memorise (Nuances that cost marks)\n\n**1. Web App Architecture: Client vs Server Processing \u0026 App Types**\nYour web dev section explains HTML/CSS/Flask syntax, but theory questions test *architecture choices*.\n*   **Missing Content:**\n    *   **Native vs Web Apps:** Web apps are platform-independent (run in browser) and easier to maintain centrally. Native apps are OS-specific but faster and work offline.\n    *   **Client-side vs Server-side Processing:** Client-side handles basic validation and UI interactivity (reduces server load). Server-side handles complex DB queries and strict security validation.\n    *   **Thin vs Thick Clients:** Thin clients rely entirely on the server for processing/storage (e.g., cheap hardware, reliant on network). Thick clients have local processing power/storage (e.g., personal laptops, work offline).\n\n**2. Network Hardware \u0026 Identifiers (Tested in Theory Q2)**\n*   **Missing Content:**\n    *   **MAC Addresses:** 48-bit (12 hex digits) hardware addresses hardcoded into the NIC. Used by **Switches** to deliver data frames on a Local Area Network (LAN). \n    *   **Internet Registries vs Registrars:** Registries (IANA, SGNIC) manage the master database and global allocation. Registrars (GoDaddy) are retail middlemen who sell domains to the public.\n    *   **Static vs Dynamic IPs:** Static is highly reliable for servers but costly. Dynamic is cost-effective (ISPs recycle them via DHCP) but unreliable for hosting.\n\n**3. OOP Theoretical Benefits (Tested in Theory Q4)**\nYour cheatsheet explains *how* to do OOP, but the exam asks *why* we do it.\n*   **Missing Content:** \n    *   **Benefits of Polymorphism:** Allows **Code Reuse** (subclasses adapt behaviors while using unified interfaces) and **Enhanced Extensibility** (adding new subclasses without modifying existing code).\n\n---\n\n### 🟢 GREEN: Useful Knowledge (Algorithm \u0026 System Details)\n\n**1. Sorting Algorithm Nuances (Tested in Theory Q2)**\nYou have the time complexities, but you missed the physical execution details.\n*   **Missing Content:**\n    *   **In-Place Sorting:** Bubble sort operates *in-place* (modifies original array). Merge Sort requires *additional memory space* due to recursive splitting.\n    *   **Early Termination:** How to optimize Bubble sort to $O(n)$ by adding a boolean flag (`noSwap`) that breaks the loop if a pass occurs with zero swaps.\n\n**2. Cloud Computing Risks \u0026 Benefits**\n*   **Missing Content:**\n    *   *Benefits:* Scalability (pay for what you use), Cost efficiency (no on-premise servers), Remote access.\n    *   *Risks:* Data privacy concerns (storing student/company data on third-party servers), Service downtime (dependency on the provider\u0027s uptime).\n\n**3. Cryptography Principles**\n*   **Missing Content:** Know the exact distinction between Encryption and Digital Signatures:\n    *   *Encryption* ensures **Confidentiality** (only authorized users can read it).\n    *   *Digital Signatures* ensure **Authentication \u0026 Integrity** (verifies the sender\u0027s identity and that the file was not altered in transit).\n\n---\n\n### 🚀 Action Plan for Your Cheatsheet\n\nTo bridge the gap between your current notes and the theory paper requirements, you should add these three specific sections to your cheatsheet:\n\n1.  **\"Compare \u0026 Contrast\" Tables:** Create tables for SQL vs NoSQL, Thin vs Thick Client, and Connection-Oriented (TCP) vs Connectionless (UDP). Theory questions love asking for 2 differences between these.\n2.  **The 4-Layer TCP/IP Diagram:** Write down the 4 layers and literally 2 bullet points of what happens at each layer. \n3.  **Malware Glossary:** A rapid-fire dictionary of Virus, Worm, Trojan, Ransomware, Spyware, Phishing, and DDoS. (See the Theory Paper Q6 solution—they give 1 mark for the name, 1 mark for how it infects).",
