      "text": "In SQL, the fundamental difference between an `INNER JOIN` and a `LEFT OUTER JOIN` is how they handle **unmatched data**:\n*   An **`INNER JOIN`** only returns records that have matching values in **both** tables. Unmatched rows are silently discarded.\n*   A **`LEFT OUTER JOIN`** returns **all** records from the left table, and the matched records from the right table. If there is no match, the right side returns `NULL` [S3C, 8].\n\nIn the real world (and in your exam), `LEFT OUTER JOIN` is used for two main practical scenarios:\n\n---\n\n### Application 1: Generating Complete Reports (The \"Do Not Hide Data\" Pattern)\nIf you need a report that lists **every single entity** from your primary table regardless of whether they have active activity records, you must use a `LEFT OUTER JOIN`.\n\n*   **The Scenario:** You want to print a list of **all students** in class `25S02X` alongside their latecoming records.\n*   **If you use `INNER JOIN`:** Students who have **never been late** will be completely omitted from your results. Your report will only show the troublemakers.\n*   **If you use `LEFT OUTER JOIN`:** \n    ```sql\n    SELECT student.Student_Name, late.Date, late.Reason\n    FROM student\n    LEFT OUTER JOIN late ON student.Student_ID \u003d late.Student_ID\n    WHERE student.Class_Group \u003d \u002725S02X\u0027\n    ```\n    *Result:* **Every** student in `25S02X` appears. Well-behaved students will simply show `NULL` under `Date` and `Reason`, which is exactly what a school administrator needs to see.\n\n---\n\n### Application 2: Finding \"Inactive\" or \"Delinquent\" Records (The `IS NULL` Pattern)\nA highly common exam question asks you to find elements that have **never** performed an action. You achieve this by pairing a `LEFT OUTER JOIN` with a `WHERE ... IS NULL` clause.\n\n*   **The Scenario:** Find all borrowers in a library database who have **never borrowed a single book**.\n*   **The Query:**\n    ```sql\n    SELECT borrower.Name \n    FROM borrower\n    LEFT OUTER JOIN Loan ON borrower.BorrowerID \u003d Loan.BorrowerID\n    WHERE Loan.LoanID IS NULL;\n    ```\n*   **How it works:** The join retains all borrowers. If a borrower has never taken a loan, their `LoanID` in the joined table is empty (`NULL`). The `WHERE` clause filters for these exact empty slots, instantly isolating your inactive users.\n\n---\n\n### Syllabus Connection\nRefer directly to **Chapter S3C, Page 8** of your notes [S3C, 8]:\n*   When executing `Book LEFT OUTER JOIN Publisher`, the book *\"H2 Computing Ten Year Series\"* is preserved in the output even though its `PublisherID` is `NULL` [S3C, 8]. \n*   An `INNER JOIN` would have completely erased that book from your catalog display [S3C, 8].",
