      "text": "Yes, your intuition is exactly right. \n\n`GROUP BY` collapses (or \"lumps\") all rows sharing the same value in your specified column into a **single, temporary summary row** [S3C, 10]. \n\nFor example, if you have 15 students spread across 3 class groups, `GROUP BY Class_Group` will collapse those 15 raw rows into exactly **3 summary rows** (one for each class) [S3C, 10].\n\n---\n\n### What is the \"Summary Record\"?\nBecause multiple rows are squeezed into one, the database engine faces a logical problem: *If 5 students are grouped into 1 row, which student\u0027s name should be displayed?* \n\nTo prevent errors, a summary record **cannot display individual row details** (unless they are part of the grouped column). Instead, the summary record can only display:\n1.  **The column you grouped by** (since every row in that group shares the exact same value).\n2.  **Calculated, mathematical summaries** of the other columns using **Aggregate Functions** (`COUNT()`, `SUM()`, `AVG()`, `MAX()`, `MIN()`) [S3C, 10, 14].\n\n---\n\n### How to Manipulate the Summary Record\nYou manipulate these computed summary records using three primary tools: `AS`, `HAVING`, and `ORDER BY` [S3C, 10].\n\n#### 1. Naming the Summary Columns (`AS`)\nSince aggregate functions create temporary, unnamed columns, use `AS` to rename them so you can reference them easily [S3C, 10].\n```sql\nSELECT Class_Group, COUNT(Student_ID) AS StudentCount\nFROM student\nGROUP BY Class_Group\n```\n\n#### 2. Filtering the Summary Records (`HAVING` vs. `WHERE`)\nThis is a high-yield exam distinction:\n*   **`WHERE`** filters individual raw rows **before** they are grouped [S3C, 13].\n*   **`HAVING`** filters the final, collapsed summary rows **after** they have been grouped [S3C, 10].\n\nIf you want to find only classes that have *more than 5* students, you must use `HAVING` because the \"count of students\" does not exist until grouping has occurred [S3C, 11].\n```sql\nSELECT Class_Group, COUNT(Student_ID) AS StudentCount\nFROM student\nGROUP BY Class_Group\nHAVING COUNT(Student_ID) \u003e 5 -- Filters out any summary row with 5 or fewer students\n```\n\n#### 3. Sorting the Summary Records (`ORDER BY`)\nYou can sort the final summary rows by your aggregated values [S3C, 11].\n```sql\nSELECT Class_Group, COUNT(Student_ID) AS StudentCount\nFROM student\nGROUP BY Class_Group\nORDER BY StudentCount DESC -- Sorts classes from most populated to least populated\n```",
