      "text": "### Part 1: Verification \u0026 Review of Your Databases Section\n\nYour Database notes are fundamentally strong. Your definitions for Normalisation (1NF atomic, 2NF no partial, 3NF no transitive) are perfectly distilled for the exam. \n\n**Here are the 3 minor inaccuracies/gaps to fix:**\n\n1.  **M:M Entity-Relationships (Clarification):** \n    *   *Your notes:* \"M:M only exists as multiple 1:M connections formed on the same junction table.\"\n    *   *Correction:* In an **E-R Diagram**, you *can and should* draw a direct M:M relationship line between two entities if asked to model it conceptually. It is only during the **Table Definition (Schema)** phase that the M:M relationship is resolved into a junction table with two 1:M relationships. \n2.  **Candidate vs. Primary Keys:**\n    *   Ensure you explicitly define a **Primary Key** as: \"A candidate key *chosen* to uniquely identify records in a table.\" (Examiners look for the word \"chosen\" or \"selected\").\n3.  **NoSQL Design Choice (Add this):**\n    *   Add a single line on how NoSQL models relationships: **Embedding** (storing related data as lists inside a single document for fast, single-query reads) vs. **Referencing** (storing IDs to link separate documents, used when data is large or frequently updated independently).\n\n---\n\n### Part 2: Crash Course on Packet Switching \u0026 TCP/IP\n\nThis is heavily tested in Paper 1. Memorise the bolded keywords.\n\n#### 1. Packet Switching (The Mechanism)\nData is not sent as a single continuous stream. It is broken into manageable chunks called **packets**.\n*   **Independence:** Each packet travels independently and may take **different routes** through the network depending on router traffic.\n*   **Anatomy of a Packet:**\n    *   **Header:** Contains Source/Destination IP, Sequence Number, and TTL (Time to Live).\n    *   **Payload:** The actual data.\n    *   **Trailer:** Contains error-checking data (Checksum/CRC).\n*   **Reassembly:** Packets may arrive out of order. They are reassembled at the destination using their **Sequence Numbers**.\n\n#### 2. Connection-Oriented vs Connectionless (The Protocols)\nPacket switching networks use two primary transport protocols:\n\n*   **TCP (Connection-Oriented):** \n    *   *How it works:* Establishes a virtual circuit (handshake) before sending.\n    *   *Traits:* **Reliable**. Uses acknowledgements. Retransmits lost packets. Guarantees ordered delivery. Slower due to overhead.\n    *   *Use case:* Web browsing (HTTP), Emails, File transfers (where data integrity is mandatory).\n*   **UDP (Connectionless):**\n    *   *How it works:* Sends packets blindly without establishing a connection.\n    *   *Traits:* **Unreliable**. No acknowledgements, no retransmissions. Fast with low overhead.\n    *   *Use case:* Live video streaming, VoIP calls, online gaming (where speed is prioritized over perfect accuracy).\n\n#### 3. The TCP/IP Model (The 4 Layers)\nData moves **down** the stack at the sender (Encapsulation: adding headers) and **up** the stack at the receiver (Decapsulation: stripping headers).\n\n| Layer | Role | Addresses Used | Key Protocols / Hardware |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **1. Application** | Provides network services directly to user software (browser/email). | *None* | HTTP, HTTPS, FTP, SMTP |\n| **2. Transport** | Handles end-to-end communication, packet sequencing, and error recovery. | **Port Numbers** (identifies the specific app) | TCP, UDP |\n| **3. Network** | Handles routing packets across different, global networks via the best path. | **IP Addresses** (identifies logical global location) | IP / **Routers** |\n| **4. Data Link** | Handles physical transmission of frames within a single Local Area Network (LAN). | **MAC Addresses** (identifies physical hardware) | Ethernet / **Switches** |\n\n#### High-Yield Exam Trap: IP vs MAC Addresses\nExaminers love asking why we need both.\n*   **IP Address (Network Layer):** End-to-end. Stays the **same** from the original sender to the final destination (like a postal address on an envelope).\n*   **MAC Address (Data Link Layer):** Hop-to-hop. Changes at **every router** along the way (like the license plate of the specific mail truck currently carrying the envelope).",
