      "text": "You are making excellent progress. The fact that you attempted to dynamically map the dictionary using `zip()` in Task 2.2 shows a very strong grasp of Python. However, you fell into a classic \"SQL mindset\" trap while working in a NoSQL environment.\n\nHere is the breakdown of what went wrong, how to patch it, and which paper you should tackle next.\n\n---\n\n### 🚨 Critical Patches for Your Code\n\n#### 1. Task 2.2: The `zip()` Trap in NoSQL\nIn SQL, every row has the exact same columns. In NoSQL, **schemas are flexible**. \n*   **Your Bug:** You captured `headers \u003d row.keys()` from the last document in Task 2.1. But in the `USERS-1.json` file, some users have `phasebookFriends` and others don\u0027t! If the last user in the JSON had 10 fields, your `headers` list has length 10. When you try to `zip()` it with your 7 input variables in Task 2.2, the lists misalign, and your data gets mapped to the wrong keys or drops entirely.\n*   **The Patch:** In NoSQL, **hardcode the dictionary keys** for data entry. It is safer, highly readable, and exactly what examiners expect.\n\n**Corrected Task 2.2:**\n```python\n# Task 2.2\nclient \u003d pymongo.MongoClient(\"mongodb://localhost:27017/\")\ndb \u003d client[\"social\"]\nusers \u003d db[\"users\"] # Use exact casing from the question paper\n\nprint(\"DATABASE ACCESS\\nType \u0027quit\u0027 as the User ID to exit.\")\n\nwhile True:\n    user_id \u003d input(\"Key in user ID: \")\n    if user_id.lower() \u003d\u003d \u0027quit\u0027:\n        break\n        \n    name \u003d input(\"Key in name: \")\n    email \u003d input(\"Key in email: \")\n    gender \u003d input(\"Key in gender (M/F): \")\n    dob \u003d input(\"Key in date of birth (dd/mm/yyyy): \")\n    \n    # Handle optional inputs with default values AND cast to integers\n    year_reg \u003d input(\"Key in year of registration (Leave blank for 2026): \")\n    year_reg \u003d int(year_reg) if year_reg else 2026\n    \n    active \u003d input(\"Key in active status (Leave blank for 1): \")\n    active \u003d int(active) if active else 1\n\n    # Create the dictionary manually (The NoSQL way)\n    new_record \u003d {\n        \"userID\": user_id,\n        \"name\": name,\n        \"email\": email,\n        \"gender\": gender,\n        \"dateOfBirth\": dob,\n        \"yearOfRegistration\": year_reg,\n        \"active\": active\n    }\n    \n    users.insert_one(new_record)\n    print(\"Successfully inserted record.\\n\")\n\nclient.close()\n```\n\n#### 2. Task 2.3: The `$exists` Operator \u0026 Data Types\nYou correctly identified how to use the list index to select the platform, but you made two fatal syntax errors that caused the compromise.\n*   **Bug 1 (Python):** `input()` returns a string. If you do `platforms[option]`, Python will crash because list indices must be integers. You must do `int(input())`.\n*   **Bug 2 (MongoDB):** You wrote `Users.find({platforms[option]})`. In MongoDB, a query *must* be a key-value dictionary. To find out if a field is \"present\" (as the question asks), you must use the **`$exists`** operator.\n\n**Corrected Task 2.3:**\n```python\n# Task 2.3\nclient \u003d pymongo.MongoClient(\"mongodb://localhost:27017/\")\nusers \u003d client[\"social\"][\"users\"]\n\nplatforms \u003d [\u0027\u0027, \"phasebookFriends\", \"viewtubeSubscribers\", \"whatsupContacts\"]\noption \u003d 0\n\nwhile True:\n    print(\"\\nDisplay Users\")\n    print(\"[1] Phasebook Users\")\n    print(\"[2] Viewtube Users\")\n    print(\"[3] Whatsup Users\")\n    print(\"[4] Quit\")\n    \n    option \u003d int(input(\"Key in the integer for your option: \")) # Cast to INT!\n    \n    if option \u003d\u003d 4:\n        break\n        \n    field_to_check \u003d platforms[option]\n    counter \u003d 0\n    \n    # Formatting the header\n    print(f\"\\n{\u0027UserID\u0027:\u003c10} | {\u0027Name\u0027:\u003c20} | Number of {field_to_check}\")\n    print(\"-\" * 55)\n    \n    # THE MONGODB FIX: Use {\"$exists\": True}\n    for result in users.find({field_to_check: {\"$exists\": True}}):\n        # Note: Use exact casing from JSON (e.g., \u0027userID\u0027, not \u0027UserID\u0027)\n        uid \u003d result[\u0027userID\u0027]\n        name \u003d result[\u0027name\u0027]\n        count \u003d result[field_to_check]\n        \n        # Use Python string formatting (\u003c10 spaces) for neat columns\n        print(f\"{uid:\u003c10} | {name:\u003c20} | {count}\")\n        counter +\u003d 1\n        \n    print(f\"\\nNumber of documents \u003d {counter}\")\n\nclient.close()\n```\n\n---\n\n### ⏭️ Your Next Target: The 2025 Paper\n\nYou should attempt the **2025 YEAR 6 TERM 3 TIMED PRACTICE** next. \n\n**Why 2025 over 2024?**\n1. **It directly targets your NoSQL weakness:** Task 2 of the 2025 paper requires you to build a MongoDB Attendance system. It will force you to use specific MongoDB queries (like finding absentees by date) and, most importantly, it tests **Updating Documents** (using `$unset` to remove fields and `$set` to add them). This will completely cure your \"SQL mindset\" when dealing with dictionaries.\n2. **It solidifies your Web Dev:** Task 1 asks you to build a single-page Flask app with an SQLite backend. This is exactly what we spent the last session reviewing. Doing this paper will lock that Flask-to-SQLite pipeline into your permanent memory.\n3. **Sockets Practice:** Task 3 is a Word Game socket program. It is an excellent test of the standard `send -\u003e receive -\u003e process` loop.\n\n**Pre-Exam Tip for the 2025 Paper:**\nWhen you get to Task 2.4 in the 2025 paper, remember your MongoDB Update boilerplate:\n`collection.update_one( {\"filter_key\": \"value\"}, {\"$set\": {\"new_key\": \"val\"}, \"$unset\": {\"old_key\": \"\"}} )`\n\nGo ahead and dive into the 2025 paper! Let me know when you\u0027ve completed it or if you get stuck on a specific task.",
