      "text": "**IP and MAC Address Spoofing**\n\nSpoofing involves falsifying network address headers to masquerade as a trusted device. It exploits a fundamental vulnerability in the TCP/IP model: receiving devices inherently trust the source address declared on a packet. \n\nHere is how spoofing applies to the **S4F Networks** and **S3G Security** syllabus.\n\n### 1. MAC Spoofing (Data Link Layer)\n*   **Mechanism:** While a MAC address is permanently hardcoded into the Network Interface Card (NIC) by the manufacturer, the operating system constructs the actual Data Link frame sent over the wire. Attackers can configure their OS to overwrite the default MAC address with a forged one before transmission.\n*   **Purpose:** MAC addresses govern local hardware delivery via switches. Attackers spoof MAC addresses to bypass MAC-filtering rules on local routers, hijack an authorized user\u0027s session on an Intranet, or conceal their device\u0027s true identity on a public Wi-Fi network.\n\n### 2. IP Spoofing (Network Layer)\n*   **Mechanism:** The attacker alters the Source IP Address within the Network layer packet header, packing it with a fake origin address.\n*   **Purpose:** IP addresses dictate logical, cross-network routing. Spoofing is used to evade IP-based firewall filtering rules by making external, malicious packets appear as if they originated from a trusted internal device.\n*   **DDoS Amplification (Syllabus Link):** IP spoofing is the engine behind Distributed Denial of Service (DDoS) attacks. An attacker sends requests to public servers using the *victim’s* spoofed IP. The servers unwittingly flood the victim with the massive responses, overwhelming the victim\u0027s bandwidth while concealing the attacker\u0027s true location.\n\n### Defending Against Spoofing (Syllabus Integration)\nBecause Network and Data Link headers are easily forged, modern security architectures cannot rely on IP or MAC addresses alone for authentication. \n\n1.  **Intrusion Detection Systems (IDS):** While basic firewalls only check packet headers (which can be spoofed), an IDS analyzes packet *payloads* and traffic patterns to flag anomalies, such as incoming packets claiming an internal IP address.\n2.  **Digital Signatures \u0026 Encryption:** Instead of trusting the Network layer, security is shifted up to the Application layer (e.g., HTTPS, VPNs). Digital signatures mathematically guarantee the sender\u0027s identity and data integrity, rendering spoofed lower-layer headers useless.",
