      "text": "**Zero Trust Architecture: Theory \u0026 Vulnerabilities**\n\nTraditional network security relies on a \"castle-and-moat\" perimeter defense: devices outside the Intranet are blocked by firewalls, but devices inside (or connected via VPN) are implicitly trusted. **Zero Trust (ZT)** discards this. It operates on the principle: *\"Never trust, always verify.\"* It assumes the network is already hostile and mandates strict identity and device verification for *every* individual request, regardless of the user\u0027s IP or MAC address.\n\nThis shifts the security paradigm from **network-level perimeter defense** (S4F) to **application-level continuous authentication** and encryption (S3G). \n\nHowever, because Zero Trust dismantles the network perimeter, it shifts the attack surface to the user\u0027s identity and device. Its vulnerabilities include:\n\n### 1. The \"Trusted Identity\" Exploit (Social Engineering)\nIn Zero Trust, identity is the new perimeter. If an attacker successfully executes a **Phishing** attack, stealing a user\u0027s credentials and intercepting their Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) tokens (e.g., via a fake login portal), the Zero Trust system will authenticate the attacker as a legitimate user. ZT cannot easily distinguish between the real user and an attacker holding valid keys.\n\n### 2. Endpoint Compromise (Malware)\nZero Trust verifies the device\u0027s compliance before granting access. However, if an authorized \"Thick Client\" (e.g., a staff laptop) is infected with a **Trojan** or **Spyware**, the malware operates under the guise of the authenticated user. The ZT architecture will authorize the device\u0027s requests, allowing the malware to exfiltrate data from within the secure session.\n\n### 3. Policy Complexity and Misconfiguration\nZero Trust relies on granular **Access Control** rules (e.g., \"User X can only access Database Y from Device Z during office hours\"). Managing thousands of micro-policies introduces severe risk of human error. A misconfigured policy can accidentally grant broad privileges, creating a vulnerability that an Intrusion Detection System (IDS) might ignore because the traffic appears \"authorized\" by the flawed rule. \n\n### 4. Insider Threats \u0026 DDoS\nZero Trust excels at stopping lateral movement (a worm spreading across a LAN), but it does not stop a disgruntled, authenticated employee from downloading and leaking data they are legitimately authorized to access. Furthermore, authentication servers become single points of failure; if attackers flood the authentication gateway via a **DDoS attack**, legitimate users cannot verify their identity, locking down the entire organization.",
